<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Victor Schoelcher</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–<a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Victor Schoelcher was a French politician and journalist who led the campaign to abolish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the French colonies. As under-secretary for the colonies in the provisional government of the Second Republic, he drafted the decree of 27 April 1848 that abolished slavery throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>, freeing about 250,000 people in Guadeloupe, Martinique, Réunion and elsewhere. He opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>, lived in exile and later became a senator.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/victor-schoelcher.jpg" alt="Victor Schoelcher" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri Decaisne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Victor_Sch%C5%93lcher_par_Henri_Decaisne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 22 July 1804, the son of Marc Schœlcher, who owned a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> factory, and Victoire Jacob. He left the Lycée Louis-le-Grand after a year to work in the family factory. In his teens he opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> monarchy while frequenting the literary and political salons of Paris.</p><p>In 1828 his father sent him on an eighteen-month trip to America as the firm’s representative. He visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, learned much about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and began writing against it. On his return in 1830 he published Des noirs in the Revue de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, which proposed gradual abolition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After inheriting the business in 1832 he sold it to devote himself to abolitionism. He went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> in 1840 to study the results of abolition in the British colonies, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> to study Muslim slavery, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> between September 1847 and January 1848. His travels convinced him to demand immediate emancipation, and in 1842 he published Des colonies françaises: Abolition immédiate de l’esclavage.</p><p>After the revolution of February 1848 he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> on 3 March. The next day François Arago, Minister of the Navy and Colonies in the provisional government, appointed him under-secretary of state for the colonies and president of a commission to draft the abolition. He argued that waiting for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> could lead to revolt and bloodshed in the colonies. He wrote the decree of 27 April 1848, which abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in all French colonies and granted citizenship to the emancipated.</p><p>He was elected deputy for Martinique in 1848 and for Guadeloupe in 1850. On 2 December 1851, the day Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte seized power, he was among the few deputies at the barricades. Exiled, he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1852, wrote a biography of Handel and published works against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>, and refused the amnesty of 1859.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in August 1870, organised an artillery legion in the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and in 1871 again sat for Martinique. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a> he tried unsuccessfully to mediate and was briefly imprisoned by the communards. He was elected senator for life in December 1875 and published a biography of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a> in 1889. He died on 25 December 1893 at Houilles, aged 89.</p><p>He left his money and collection to Guadeloupe, now in the Schœlcher Museum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pointe-a-pitre">Pointe-à-Pitre</a>. His remains were moved to the Panthéon on 20 May 1949, together with those of his father and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-eboue">Félix Éboué</a>. The commune of Case-Navire in Martinique took his name in 1888. Statues of him in Martinique were destroyed in 2020 and 2021 in protests against colonial memory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Victor Schœlcher” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1848 France, by a decree drafted by <b>Victor Schoelcher</b>, and Denmark, after a rising on St Croix, freed the enslaved in their colonies.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Slavery was finally abolished in 1848 through <b>Victor Schoelcher's</b> decree, after which indentured workers came from India.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-guadeloupe">Colony of Guadeloupe</a></li><li><blockquote>A provisional government including the poet Alphonse de Lamartine proclaimed the Republic, introduced universal male suffrage, abolished slavery in the colonies under <b>Victor Schoelcher's</b> decree, and created National Workshops for the unemployed.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-second-republic">French Second Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1861" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Victor Schoelcher"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1861">What was here in 1861? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
